meta { title = "GPU numerical graphs", description = "Compose resident numerical graphs with strict types and shapes.", order = 3, group = "HOWTO guides" }

Once you can run the first GPU example, combine numerical operations inside a function placed on that device. Keep intermediate tensors on the GPU and download only the values the CPU needs. The compiler may use several kernels or submissions for one function.

The examples below use the Metal commands shown on this page. For Vulkan or CUDA, use the corresponding backend commands and prerequisites in the GPU guide.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/gpu", "First GPU program and backend-specific build commands" }
}

h2 { "Row math for attention" }

transpose, softmaxRows, and rmsNormRows compose with matrix multiplication inside numerical GPU programs. Transpose preserves bits for all ten numeric types. The two row-math operations currently require hardware float32 on Metal and experimental Vulkan/CUDA; their CPU implementations also support hardware float64. RMS normalization takes an explicit finite, positive epsilon of the same type as its tensor. It has unit gain. These operations do not move data between CPU and GPU implicitly.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn normalize(scores: Tensor[float32, ?, ?]) -> Tensor[float32, ?, ?]:
  softmaxRows(scores)

fn main() -> float32 ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    cpu = devices.cpu()
    scores: Tensor[float32, 2, 3] = tensor([[1, 2, 3], [-1, 0, 1]])
    a = try await transfer(scores, gpu)
    b = try await spawn normalize(a) on gpu
    result = try await transfer(b, cpu)
    reduceSum(result)
""" }

The numerical result is approximately 2: each row sums to approximately one. Intermediate outputs stay alive until the device has finished using them. Composing operations avoids unnecessary transfers, but does not guarantee a single kernel or command submission. Softmax uses row maxima for stability and an approximation to the exponential function; CPU and GPU results need not be bit-identical.

h2 { "Compose two matrix products" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn multiply(a: Tensor[float32, ?, ?], b: Tensor[float32, ?, ?]) -> Tensor[float32, ?, ?]:
  matmul(a, b)

fn project(input: Tensor[float32, ?, ?], weights: Tensor[float32, ?, ?], projection: Tensor[float32, ?, ?]) -> Tensor[float32, ?, ?]:
  hidden = multiply(input, weights)
  multiply(hidden, projection)

fn main() -> Tensor[float32, ?, ?] ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    input: Tensor[float32, 2, 3] = tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
    weights: Tensor[float32, 3, 2] = tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]])
    projection: Tensor[float32, 2, 2] = tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
    a = try await transfer(input, gpu)
    b = try await transfer(weights, gpu)
    c = try await transfer(projection, gpu)
    work = spawn project(a, b, c) on gpu
    result = try await work
    cached = try await work
    try await transfer(cached, devices.cpu())
""" }

Save the complete example as main.k and run it with the mixed CPU/Metal toolchain:

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra main.k --target metal -O2 -o app
./app/program
""" }

The numerical result is the row-major matrix [[106, 156], [241, 354]]. The float32 Metal executable displays [42d40000,431c0000,43710000,43b10000], the corresponding hexadecimal bit patterns.

The two ordinary helper calls form a resident graph. In this example the input is 2 by 3, weights are 3 by 2, and projection is 2 by 2. The final result is 2 by 2. Awaiting work twice reuses the completed tensor; only the final explicit transfer downloads it.

h2 { "Prepare inputs with arrays" }

Use numeric and Bool arrays to build and edit data on the CPU side of a GPU program. Local reassignment, map, generate, copy, sorting and element writes follow the same alias and task-reader rules as CPU programs. CPU helpers can receive and return arrays, including across task waits.

Convert a numeric array to a tensor before transferring it to a GPU. tensor(values) copies the data, so later array edits cannot change the tensor snapshot or a running GPU calculation.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn twice(values: Tensor[float32, ?]) -> Tensor[float32, ?]:
  values + values

fn main() -> float32 ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    values = generate(3, fn(index): float32(index + 1))
    snapshot = tensor(values)
    values[0] = float32(9)
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    resident = try await transfer(snapshot, gpu)
    work = spawn twice(resident) on gpu
    result = try await transfer(try await work, devices.cpu())
    reduceSum(result) + values[0]
""" }

This returns 21, displayed as 41a80000. The GPU doubles the original [1, 2, 3] snapshot; the CPU adds the edited first array element, 9. array(tensor) also copies, so editing that array cannot change the tensor.

This example prepares arrays on the host and transfers an immutable tensor snapshot. GPU helpers can also construct and modify their own numeric/Bool arrays, as described below. Arrays cannot yet be passed across a CPU/device entry boundary. Arrays of Strings, records, enums or function values, first-class function values and parallelMap are not supported as coordinating values in mixed GPU programs.

h2 { "Types and ranks" }

The supported numerical types are int8, uint8, int16, uint16, int32, uint32, int64, uint64, float32 and float64. Dense tensors of rank zero through eight are supported by the numerical operations that accept those ranks. matmul remains rank two. Numeric identity and complete shape obligations are checked rather than implicitly promoted or broadcast.

h2 { "Hardware arithmetic and explicit fallback" }

Float32 uses hardware arithmetic on CPU, Metal and the experimental Vulkan/CUDA backends by default. CUDA also uses native float64 on supported hardware. Integer arithmetic retains overflow checks. Apple Metal has no native float64 arithmetic on the supported target; compiling float64 GPU arithmetic requires the explicit option --arithmetic hardware-with-software-fallback. Under that policy, CPU float64 and GPU float32 stay on hardware, while GPU float64 uses integer-based software arithmetic on the GPU.

Use --arithmetic software for the strict software implementation on both CPU and GPU. This selects software arithmetic with gradual underflow and its defined numerical contract. Default hardware mode follows device behavior, so Metal float32 subnormals may flush to zero. reduceSum keeps its balanced adjacent-pair tree, and scalar arithmetic retains its separately rounded operations. -O2 does not enable generic fast math.

h2 { "Matrix precision policies" }

Metal hardware float32 matmul defaults to optimized full-precision accumulation. Experimental Vulkan and CUDA default to fixed-tree and require explicit --matmul-policy optimized for their tiled kernels. The compiler may use fused multiply-add and choose a contraction order suited to the device while preserving float32 inputs and accumulators. This does not silently convert inputs to float16 or TF32.

For eligible aligned float32 products, CUDA's optimized path splits the contraction into five independent GPU partitions and reduces them deterministically. It keeps full float32 inputs and accumulators. Temporary workspace counts against the device budget; if that workspace cannot fit, a slower GPU route computes the same accumulation order. Memory pressure does not select a different numerical result. Unsupported shapes keep the fixed-tree GPU companion.

Use --matmul-policy fixed-tree to require separately rounded products and the existing balanced contraction tree. This selects a calculation order, not exact real arithmetic or universally greater accuracy. Optimized results may differ in rounding, signed zero and intermediate overflow. Subtraction of nearly equal large terms, called numerical cancellation, can lose accuracy in either mode; it is unrelated to task cancellation. Checked integer operations are unchanged, and CPU matmul currently retains the fixed tree under either policy.

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra main.k --target metal --matmul-policy fixed-tree -O2 -o app
./app/program
""" }

Hardware fixed-tree mode still follows the device's underflow and NaN behavior. Use --arithmetic software when the software numerical contract, including gradual underflow, is required; that option always selects fixed-tree matmul.

h2 { "Conditions in GPU functions" }

Eligible GPU functions can use numeric comparisons, Boolean expressions and conditions to choose work. Only a selected branch runs, including its shape checks. Boolean conditions do not make Bool tensors, Strings or arbitrary host objects valid device data. Device and task resource limits still apply.

GPU functions can accept Bool scalars and return Bool results. Awaiting a Bool task produces a Bool, not a numeric byte; numeric and Bool arguments are not interchangeable. A Bool main prints true or false, while numerical GPU results keep their hexadecimal bit display.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn oddAndEnabled(enabled: Bool, value: int) -> Bool:
  enabled and value % 2 == 1

fn main() -> Bool ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    result = spawn oddAndEnabled(true, 7) on gpu
    first = try await result
    second = try await result
    first and second
""" }

This prints true. Repeated awaits observe the same completed value. The same Bool result type works for CPU tasks and for coordinating helpers that await GPU work. Bounds/arithmetic failures and cancellation remain task failures; they are not converted into false.

h2 { "Scalar loops on the GPU" }

Numeric and Bool scalar helpers can use local numeric and Bool state, while loops, for loops over range(...), break and continue on the GPU. Both single-result loop expressions and statement loops are supported. Call these helpers from numerical GPU functions or place a numeric or Bool scalar entry directly on the device.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn countSteps(count: int) -> float32:
  total: float32 = 0
  for index in range(count):
    total = total + float32(1)
  total

fn main() -> float32 ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn countSteps(1000) on gpu
""" }

This computes 1000 on the GPU. The executable prints its float32 bits as 447a0000. Loop arithmetic and branch decisions execute on the device. Each scalar helper runs sequentially in one GPU thread; placing it on a GPU does not parallelize its iterations. The runtime saves private scalar state between bounded chunks and checks cancellation before submitting more work. There is no total iteration limit; execution remains subject to ordinary device, task and memory limits. A cancellation request cannot reclaim buffers until submitted work has finished.

Helpers with numeric or Bool results can use numeric, Bool, plain-data record/enum and local numeric/Bool array state. Tensor values carried around a loop and recursive device calls are not supported. Keep tensor operations outside those loops and compose them with scalar helpers. Numeric policies still apply; Metal float64 arithmetic requires explicit software arithmetic or hardware-with-software-fallback.

h2 { "Local arrays and collecting loops" }

A GPU helper can construct numeric and Bool arrays, index and iterate them, and modify local elements. Assignment keeps the same backing; copy creates independent element storage. slice and concat also construct independent arrays. A synchronous helper with a mut parameter can modify an array created by the calling GPU helper. GPU tasks can capture existing numeric and Bool arrays as read-only parameters. Helpers can return flat numeric or Bool arrays as native CPU arrays after await.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn selected(n: int) -> int:
  indices = generate(n, fn(i): i)
  values = for i in indices:
    if i % 2 == 1:
      for offset in range(2):
        yield i * 10 + offset
  snapshot = copy(values)
  values[0] = 999
  total = 0
  ordered = sorted(map(snapshot, fn(value): value))
  for value in ordered:
    total = total + value
  total

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn selected(10) on gpu
""" }

This computes 505 and prints its int64 bits as 00000000000001f9. yield appends in lexical order; filtering, nested loops, break, continue and empty results keep their ordinary CPU semantics. Array operations and collection run on the GPU, sequentially within the helper invocation. These arrays do not become shared append buffers or implicitly parallel loops.

generate calls a callback with each index; array map calls it with each input element. Both visit indices in increasing order and allocate independent output storage before callbacks run. Named nonrecursive helpers and callback literals with supported captured values run on-device, including callbacks that use loops and local arrays. An empty input makes no callback calls; a negative generate count produces a checked bounds fault.

Local arrays use invocation-owned device memory. Their backing can grow as needed, subject to device and task memory budgets. Unreachable numeric/Bool arrays are reclaimed during execution, so repeatedly constructing temporary arrays does not retain every earlier allocation. Allocation, copying and collection have cancellation checkpoints. Checked bounds errors discard partial results, and submitted work settles before its storage is released.

The numeric and Bool array representation uses a 64-bit cell per element plus private allocation metadata. Memory costs can therefore exceed the corresponding CPU array payload. Strings, records and function values are not supported as device-array elements. sort orders a local numeric/Bool array in place, so aliases observe the changes. sorted allocates independent backing first. Both use the same deterministic order as CPU arrays, including NaNs and signed zero, and compare floating values by integer keys without floating-point arithmetic. Sorting has cancellation checkpoints between bounded comparison/swap steps.

h2 { "Records and enums in GPU helpers" }

A device helper can construct records and enum variants containing numeric scalars, Bool, numeric/Bool arrays and nested records. Field access, record updates, value copies and match execute on the device, including inside loops. Internal nonrecursive helpers can accept and return those values.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """type Sample:
  value: float32
  enabled: Bool

fn adjusted(value: float32) -> float32:
  sample = Sample {value: value, enabled: true}
  if sample.enabled:
    sample.value + float32(2)
  else:
    float32(0)

fn main() -> float32 ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn adjusted(float32(40)) on gpu
""" }

This computes 42; the executable prints 42280000. Records are copied by value within the private device frame. Inactive enum payloads are zeroed, and fields retain their declared numeric types. Record array fields preserve their backing aliases. Use copy on an array field when you need independent element storage. These are local device values: records cannot cross the host/device entry boundary. Strings, tensors, function values and recursive records are not supported as their device fields. Scalar entry parameters and results can be numeric or Bool.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """type Batch:
  values: [int]

fn sumBatch(count: int) -> int:
  batch = Batch {values: generate(count, fn(index): index + 1)}
  total = 0
  for value in batch.values:
    total = total + value
  total

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn sumBatch(4) on gpu
""" }

This computes 10 and prints 000000000000000a. The array is constructed and read on the GPU. Its backing remains alive while batch refers to it, including across helper calls and allocation checkpoints.

h2 { "Capture host arrays" }

Pass a numeric or Bool array directly to a GPU-placed helper. The task captures the array as read-only, packs and uploads its elements, then executes the helper on the GPU. Packing belongs to the spawned task; spawn does not wait for it. The original array and its aliases stay read-locked until the task settles. An empty array is valid.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn firstAndOriginal(values: [int]) -> int:
  ordered = sorted(values)
  ordered[0] + values[0]

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  values: [int] = [5, 3, 1]
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn firstAndOriginal(values) on gpu
""" }

This computes 6 and prints 0000000000000006. sorted creates independent device storage; values remains unchanged. Captured arrays follow the same read-only rules as ordinary function parameters. Use copy or sorted before modifying elements inside the GPU helper. Writing through a host alias while the task is pending is a compile-time error.

Array capture supports all ten numeric types and Bool, subject to the selected arithmetic policy. Packing and upload costs count toward end-to-end latency, and their storage counts toward task and device budgets. The private wire representation uses a 64-bit cell per element, so it can use more memory than the corresponding CPU array. Captured host arrays and resident tensor inputs cannot yet be combined in one GPU entry. Return numeric/Bool scalars or flat numeric/Bool arrays. Record results cannot cross this boundary.

h2 { "Return arrays from GPU tasks" }

A GPU helper can return a numeric or Bool array. Awaiting it gives the caller a native CPU array. The helper's computation runs on the GPU; returning its array includes device-to-host transfer and decoding costs. Empty results are valid, and their element type comes from the function's result type.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn ordered(values: [int]) -> [int]:
  sorted(values)

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  values: [int] = [9, 2, 5]
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    result = try await spawn ordered(values) on gpu
    result[0] = 7
    result[0] + values[0]
""" }

This computes 16 and prints 0000000000000010. The GPU sorts into independent storage, so editing result leaves values unchanged. A helper returning a captured array directly preserves its original CPU backing instead: assignment and helper calls do not imply a copy. Other pending readers still prevent writes through any alias. Use copy or sorted when you need independent backing.

Generators do not need an input array: a GPU helper can return the array produced by a for/while expression or generate. Result order follows the corresponding CPU operation. A returned array can be read, modified under the normal access rules, or captured by another GPU task. For repeated calculations on resident tensors, use explicit tensor transfers instead of moving an array back after each task.

main can return the array directly:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn squares(n: int) -> [int]:
  generate(n, fn(i): i * i)

fn main() -> [int] ! TaskError:
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn squares(4) on gpu
""" }

The values are [0, 1, 4, 9]. Like other numerical output from a GPU-targeted executable, numeric array cells print as fixed-width hexadecimal bit patterns: [0000000000000000,0000000000000001,0000000000000004,0000000000000009]. Bool array cells print as true or false.


Input packing, temporary device storage, result transfer and native result storage count toward their respective resource budgets. Transfers and decoding proceed in bounded pages with cancellation checks. A bounds fault, allocation failure or cancellation discards partial results; submitted copies settle before their backing is released.

h2 { "AI and LLM foundations" }

Matrix products, tensor arithmetic, conversions and reductions are numerical building blocks for AI workloads. Kalandra does not supply model loading, tokenizers or a full LLM inference runtime, and the Linux CUDA backend remains experimental. Start with a small numerical graph and measure both resident execution and transfer-inclusive latency.

h2 { "Keep records in coordinating CPU code" }

CPU helpers and CPU tasks can receive and return records and enums while the program also runs GPU work. Their arrays keep normal alias identity, including when returned through a CPU task. Recursive links, String fields and record-array fields can remain in these CPU values. Writes through an array field or its alias are rejected while a task is reading it; an independent copy remains writable.

Pass an individual supported array field to a GPU helper, then wrap the returned native array in a CPU record:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """type Batch:
  values: [int]

fn squares(values: [int]) -> [int]:
  generate(length(values), fn(i): values[i] * values[i])

fn summarize(batch: Batch) -> int:
  batch.values[0] + batch.values[1]

fn main() -> int ! TaskError:
  original = Batch {values: [2, 3]}
  try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    values = try await spawn squares(original.values) on gpu
    result = Batch {values: values}
    summarize(result)
""" }

This computes 13 and prints 000000000000000d. squares executes on the GPU; the records and summarize execute on the CPU. This passes an array across the boundary, not the enclosing record. Passing or returning an entire record through a GPU task still requires aggregate device transport and is rejected.

h2 { "CPU and device language coverage" }

Device support means the operation executes on the selected GPU. A host continuation may schedule GPU work between CPU operations; that does not turn the CPU operations into device instructions. The portable device column applies to Metal, Vulkan and CUDA, subject to the numerical policies and backend limits above. Windows GPU execution remains experimental and physically unqualified.

